WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.320
Two species left no fossil of the moment they split.

2
00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:09.720
Can their genes still tell you when it happened?

3
00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:13.980
Line up the same gene from both, and some sites differ.

4
00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:17.430
Start with one ancestral population.

5
00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:22.310
It splits into two lineages, and each keeps gaining mutations.

6
00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:26.890
Some spread until every member of the lineage carries them.

7
00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:30.910
A mutation that has spread like that is a substitution.

8
00:00:32.133 --> 00:00:36.953
Substitutions build up on both branches, starting from the common ancestor.

9
00:00:38.353 --> 00:00:42.793
So the differences you count between two species come from both branches added

10
00:00:42.793 --> 00:00:43.793
together.

11
00:00:44.617 --> 00:00:48.777
If substitutions pile up at a steady pace, their count measures time.

12
00:00:50.057 --> 00:00:51.837
But why should the pace be steady at all?

13
00:00:52.997 --> 00:00:55.017
The answer lies inside a population.

14
00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:59.590
Picture two populations, one small and one large.

15
00:01:00.830 --> 00:01:03.510
Every generation, each copies the same gene.

16
00:01:04.810 --> 00:01:06.630
Follow only the neutral mutations.

17
00:01:07.990 --> 00:01:10.650
They make no difference to survival or reproduction.

18
00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:15.280
Suppose the large population were ten times bigger.

19
00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:19.660
It makes ten times more new mutations each generation.

20
00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:23.780
Does it pile up substitutions ten times faster?

21
00:01:30.283 --> 00:01:36.123
No. Each new neutral mutation starts as one copy among many.

22
00:01:37.383 --> 00:01:42.483
In the large population, its chance of spreading to everyone is smaller, by the same

23
00:01:42.483 --> 00:01:47.343
factor. More mutations, but each one is less likely to spread.

24
00:01:48.417 --> 00:01:49.897
The two effects cancel.

25
00:01:51.257 --> 00:01:56.637
On average, neutral mutations become substitutions at the rate they arise, whatever

26
00:01:56.637 --> 00:01:57.837
the population size.

27
00:01:59.197 --> 00:02:01.137
That steady input makes the clock tick.

28
00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:04.560
Now try to read a count as time.

29
00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:08.820
One distance could mean a fast rate over a short time.

30
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:12.640
It could also mean a slow rate over a long time.

31
00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:15.980
The sequences alone cannot tell which.

32
00:02:16.983 --> 00:02:19.403
You need one date from outside the sequences.

33
00:02:20.803 --> 00:02:22.223
The commonest is a fossil.

34
00:02:23.523 --> 00:02:27.023
If two splits share a rate, one dated split can date the other.

35
00:02:28.117 --> 00:02:31.297
But a fossil only shows the group existed by then.

36
00:02:32.517 --> 00:02:35.297
The real split was earlier, by an unknown margin.

37
00:02:36.457 --> 00:02:38.257
So a fossil gives a minimum age.

38
00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:41.060
One limit already matters.

39
00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:43.960
The clock counts neutral changes.

40
00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:50.100
A mutation removed by selection never becomes a substitution, so a tightly

41
00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:51.820
constrained gene ticks slowly.

42
00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:56.980
So neutral changes pile up, on average, at the rate they arise.

43
00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:01.760
Count them, add one dated split, and the count becomes time.
